{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>That He may establish you this day for a people to Himself.</b> So much does He trouble Himself — to establish you before Him as a people." "<b>And He will be to you for an Elohim.</b> Because He spoke to you and swore to your fathers not to exchange their seed for another nation, therefore He binds you with these oaths — that you not provoke Him, since He cannot separate Himself from you. Up to here I have explained the plain sense of the passage. And its midrash aggadah: Why is the passage 'You are standing' juxtaposed to the curses? Because Israel heard ninety-eight curses, besides the forty-nine in the Torah of the Priests, their faces turned pale and they said: Who can endure these? Moses began to pacify them — <b>You are standing this day</b>: you have provoked the Omnipresent many times, yet He has not done annihilation to you, and here you are, standing before Him." "<b>This day.</b> Like this day — which stands and darkens and illuminates — so He illuminated for you, and so He is destined to illuminate for you; and the curses and the sufferings sustain you and establish you before Him. And even the passage above this one is words of pacification: 'You have seen all that He did.' Another matter — <b>You are standing</b>: because Israel were passing from leader to leader — from Moses to Joshua — therefore he made them a standing assembly in order to stir them. And so did Joshua do (Joshua 24), and so did Samuel (I Samuel 12): 'Present yourselves and I will judge you' — when they departed from his hand and entered the hand of Saul (see Tanhuma)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:48:52.054241258Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}